("RDG","RDW","near","Greyfriars Church, Reading",L,51.45679,-0.97646,300,"Your train passes near Greyfriars Church, on the train's {side}, the oldest Franciscan church in the UK still used for worship.","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greyfriars_Church,_Reading",X),
("RDW","THE","near","Garston Lock",L,51.43193,-1.05798,350,"Your train passes near Garston Lock, on the train's {side}, one of only two working turf-sided locks left on the Kennet and Avon Canal. Its sloping grass sides use more water than brick ones.","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garston_Lock",X),
("THE","AMT","near","Folly Farm, Sulhamstead",L,51.41578,-1.09425,750,"Your train passes near Folly Farm, on the train's {side}, a house that Edwin Lutyens extended around 1906 and 1912, with gardens he designed with Gertrude Jekyll.","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Folly_Farm,_Sulhamstead",X),
("AMT","MDG","near","Aldermaston Lock",L,51.40020,-1.13747,200,"Your train passes near Aldermaston Lock, on the train's {side}, built between 1718 and 1723 and later given unusual scalloped brick walls.","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldermaston_Lock",X),
("MDG","THA","near","Midgham Lock",L,51.39220,-1.21378,150,"Your train passes near Midgham Lock, on the train's {side}, one of the locks built between 1718 and 1723 under the engineer John Hore of Newbury, when the River Kennet was made navigable.","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midgham_Lock",X),
("THA","NRC","near","Monkey Marsh Lock",L,51.39262,-1.24786,100,"Your train passes near Monkey Marsh Lock, on the train's {side}, a turf-sided lock from the 1720s that is now a scheduled monument.","https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1006971",X),
("NRC","NBY","near","West Berkshire Museum",R,51.40129,-1.32224,400,"Your train passes near the West Berkshire Museum, on the train's {side}, partly housed in Newbury's Cloth Hall, built in 1627 as a cloth factory.","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Berkshire_Museum",X),
("NBY","KIT","near","Litten Chapel",L,51.39654,-1.32668,100,"Your train passes near the Litten Chapel, on the train's {side}, an early 16th-century chapel of Newbury's medieval St Bartholomew's hospital, with finely carved timber roof trusses.","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Litten_Chapel",X),
("KIT","HGD","near","Church of St Mark and St Luke, Avington",R,51.40948,-1.46543,350,"Your train passes near the church of St Mark and St Luke at Avington, on the train's {side}, an unspoilt Norman church listed Grade I.","https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1319539",X),
